# Commands run after this gateway is up and before anything is measured. One per line.
#
# WHY THIS GATEWAY HAS THEM AND ALMOST NONE DO. It has no config file. It keeps its configuration in
# a database and is configured through its own admin API after it boots, so there is nothing to drop
# next to the binary. These lines are the deployment its own documentation describes: log in, raise
# the quota so a throughput run cannot exhaust it, wire each upstream as a channel pointing at the
# mock, then check that the credential the harness will authenticate with actually works.
#
# The channel type selects the native egress dialect: 1 is OpenAI, 14 is Anthropic, 24 is Gemini.
# The model lists are strictly disjoint on purpose. Routing selects on (group, model) and not on
# channel type, so overlapping lists would let one model resolve to either channel at random and a
# column would silently measure an upstream it did not ask for.
curl -sf -c /tmp/oa.jar -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/user/login -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"username":"root","password":"123456"}' -o /dev/null
curl -sf -b /tmp/oa.jar -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/user/ -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"id":1,"username":"root","display_name":"Root User","role":100,"status":1,"quota":1000000000000,"group":"default"}' -o /dev/null
curl -sf -b /tmp/oa.jar -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/channel/ -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"mock-1","type":1,"key":"sk-mock","base_url":"http://127.0.0.1:8000","models":"gpt-4o-mini","group":"default","status":1}' -o /dev/null
curl -sf -b /tmp/oa.jar -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/channel/ -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"mock-14","type":14,"key":"sk-mock","base_url":"http://127.0.0.1:8000","models":"claude-3-5-sonnet-20240620","group":"default","status":1}' -o /dev/null
curl -sf -b /tmp/oa.jar -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/channel/ -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"name":"mock-24","type":24,"key":"sk-mock","base_url":"http://127.0.0.1:8000","models":"gemini-1.5-pro","group":"default","status":1}' -o /dev/null
# THE RELAY CREDENTIAL IS A BOOT-TIME ONE, NOT A MINTED ONE. One-API's AddToken handler
# (controller/token.go) ignores any client-supplied "key" and always writes a random one of its own,
# and UpdateToken discards "key" too - so no admin-API call can make the manifest's declared auth
# valid by asking for it by name. Minting one and handing the real key back to the engine works for
# the in-process probes and ONLY for those: the load generator is a child process and carries its own
# credential, so a server-chosen key that only the parent knows leaves every load window
# authenticating as something one-api never issued and failing 100% while the probe beside it passes.
#
# INITIAL_ROOT_TOKEN (env, see this gateway's `env` file) is the one path that fixes that, because it
# makes the credential the manifest DECLARES the real one: on a fresh database model/main.go creates
# the root user's token with that literal string as its key, unlimited quota, no expiry and no model
# restriction. Everything downstream then authenticates with the same declared value every other
# gateway here uses, with nothing to carry between processes. Not to be confused with
# INITIAL_ROOT_ACCESS_TOKEN, which is the admin-API system token and does NOT authorize relay routes.
#
# Verify it rather than assume it: an image that stopped honouring the variable would otherwise be
# published as a gateway that serves nothing. One `commands` line is one independent `/bin/sh -c`
# (manifest.rs's loader), so the retry has to be a single line - `;` joined, not real newlines. The
# retry covers the channel rows this file just inserted not yet being visible to the router. No -f on
# the curl: the 401 body is exactly what this loop inspects, and -f discards it on any non-2xx.
#
# EVERY MODEL, NOT JUST THE FIRST. This checked gpt-4o-mini alone while creating three channels, so a
# channel that inserted but never became routable was invisible here and surfaced later as the
# GATEWAY failing: one-api published "no available channel for model gemini-1.5-pro" as two red cells
# in all eight runs of 2026-07-27/28, including the oldest engine. A setup this harness got wrong must
# stop the run and say so, not become a verdict about the gateway. The channel POSTs above already
# abort on a non-zero exit (bin/otb.rs runs each line and stops the run on failure); this closes the
# gap where the POST succeeds and the routing still does not work.
for m in gpt-4o-mini claude-3-5-sonnet-20240620 gemini-1.5-pro; do i=1; ok=0; while [ $i -le 20 ]; do curl -s -m 10 -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/v1/chat/completions -H 'content-type: application/json' -H 'Authorization: Bearer dummy' -d "{\"model\":\"$m\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"hi\"}],\"max_tokens\":1}" -o /tmp/oa-auth-check; if grep -q chat.completion /tmp/oa-auth-check; then ok=1; break; fi; sleep 0.5; i=$((i+1)); done; [ "$ok" = 1 ] || { echo "one-api: model $m never relayed after 20 checks - its channel is registered but not routable, so every cell for that egress would publish a red this harness caused: $(cat /tmp/oa-auth-check)" >&2; exit 1; }; done
